   "args": {
    "text": "Verdict: not supported\n\nThe cited source in the library, Olah et al. (2020) (*Zoom In: An Introduction to Circuits*), does not support the claim that mechanistic analysis \"has been shown to fully explain the behavior of entire neural networks\".\n\nRather than claiming to fully explain the behavior of entire neural networks, Olah et al. (2020) contrast their approach with work that attempts to explain entire network behavior, noting: \"Most work on interpretability aims to give simple explanations of an entire neural network’s behavior. But what if we instead take an approach inspired by neuroscience or cellular biology — an approach of zooming in?\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b7). They observe that \"it’s very difficult to make robustly true statements about the behavior of a neural network as a whole\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b129). Consequently, their approach sidesteps this challenge by focusing on small subgraphs: \"Circuits sidestep these challenges by focusing on tiny subgraphs of a neural network for which rigorous empirical investigation is tractable\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b130), acknowledging that \"the cost of this rigor is that statements about circuits are much smaller in scope than overall model behavior\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b130). Furthermore, they explicitly state: \"But the truth is that we’ve only scratched the surface of understanding a single vision model\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b11).\n\n(Note that the sentence in question comes from Shevlane et al. (2023), *Model evaluation for extreme risks* (arxiv:2305.15324/v2/p12), which in the original document only states: \"Another important tool is mechanistic analysis, i.e. studying the model’s weights and activations for understanding how it functions (Nanda et al., 2023; Olah et al., 2020).\" without claiming that it fully explains entire neural networks. The second cited paper, Nanda et al. (2023), is not present in the library.)"
   },
