    "text": "Verdict: not supported\n\nThe cited source present in the library, Olah et al. (2020) (*Zoom In: An Introduction to Circuits*), does not support the claim that mechanistic analysis has been shown to fully explain the behavior of entire neural networks.\n\nRather than claiming to explain the behavior of entire networks, Olah et al. (2020) contrast their approach with such goals, writing: \"Most work on interpretability aims to give simple explanations of an entire neural network’s behavior. But what if we instead take an approach inspired by neuroscience or cellular biology — an approach of zooming in?\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b7). They explain that \"it’s very difficult to make robustly true statements about the behavior of a neural network as a whole\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b129). Instead of analyzing whole networks, they study circuits, noting: \"Circuits sidestep these challenges by focusing on tiny subgraphs of a neural network for which rigorous empirical investigation is tractable\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b130). They acknowledge that \"the cost of this rigor is that statements about circuits are much smaller in scope than overall model behavior\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b130), and emphasize: \"But the truth is that we’ve only scratched the surface of understanding a single vision model\" (distill:zoom-in/r1/b11).\n\nAdditionally, the sentence presented in the prompt is an altered version of a sentence in Shevlane et al. (2023), *Model evaluation for extreme risks*, which actually reads: \"Another important tool is mechanistic analysis, i.e. studying the model’s weights and activations for understanding how it functions (Nanda et al., 2023; Olah et al., 2020).\" (arxiv:2305.15324/v2/p12). The other cited source, Nanda et al. (2023), is cited in the references of Shevlane et al. (arxiv:2305.15324/v2/p17) as *Progress measures for grokking via mechanistic interpretability*, but is not present in the library."
